<https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entries/mobus-2022-tamd>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/atlas-core> ;
    rdfs:label "Atlas entry — Mobus 2022, Systems Science: Theory, Analysis, Modeling, and Design"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Entry 006. The first entry drafted by the P5 ingest pipeline (2026-08-17)
and promoted through a guided human pass (2026-08-18) — verbatim, spans, caveats, location,
primitives and cases each ruled on individually. The published definition is a SEVEN-tuple
with no environment element; the eighth element arrives in the revisions manuscript
(bearer registered as mobus-book-revisions, entry to come), which is why this bearer and
that one stay distinct: the revision arc is the finding, as with Bertalanffy 1968/1972."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Bearer
##############################################################################

bearer:mobus-2022-tamd
    a cco:ont00000253 ;   # Information Bearing Entity
    rdfs:label "Mobus, G. E. — Systems Science: Theory, Analysis, Modeling, and Design (Springer, 2022)"@en ;
    dcterms:creator "George E. Mobus" ;
    atlas:authoredBy author:george-mobus ;
    dcterms:date "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dcterms:identifier "ISBN 978-3-030-93481-1" ;
    dcterms:identifier "doi:10.1007/978-3-030-93482-8" .


##############################################################################
# Entry 006 — the published seven-tuple
##############################################################################

entry:mobus-2022-seven-tuple
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Mobus (2022), Systems Science: TAMD, eq. (4.1)"@en ;

    atlas:statedIn bearer:mobus-2022-tamd ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 4, §4.3.3, eq. (4.1), p. 191" ;

    atlas:verbatim """Deriving a definition of system from the principles and the ontology, a system S is a 7-tuple:
S_i, l = C, N, G, B, T, H, Δt_i, l
where i and l are indexes. The index i is a subsystem index and the index l is the level of organization in the system-subsystem hierarchy. Both are 0 for the initial system of interest, S_0,0 is the designated SOI.""" ;

    # Presentation spans — exact substrings of the verbatim, enforced by the build.
    atlas:displayForm "S_i, l = C, N, G, B, T, H, Δt_i, l" ;
    atlas:displayContext "where i and l are indexes. The index i is a subsystem index and the index l is the level of organization in the system-subsystem hierarchy. Both are 0 for the initial system of interest, S_0,0 is the designated SOI." ;

    # Two caveats, both the author's own. The first stands at §4.3's opening and
    # qualifies all three presentational forms of the definition (verbal,
    # graphical, mathematical); the second is footnote 7, hung off the end of
    # §4.3. Ruled to carry both, 2026-08-18.
    atlas:authorCaveat "The following definition is proposed as a starting point for developing a formal definition of system." ;
    atlas:authorCaveat "There is a point we need to be clear about. What is being presented here is, itself, a concept about what a system consists of, how it is composed. It is not being represented as the \"general theory of systems,\" though it might be a candidate for that title." ;

    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:subsystem ,
                           prim:level-of-organization ,
                           prim:hierarchy ,
                           prim:system-of-interest ,
                           prim:time ;

    atlas:admits case:mobus-atom ;

    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; checked by Shingai Thornton (guided pass)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-18"^^xsd:date ;

    rdfs:comment """VERIFIED AS MDHC: model-drafted by the P5 pipeline 2026-08-17, checked by
Shingai Thornton 2026-08-18 in a guided session against the vault digitisation — verbatim,
spans, both caveats and their positions, location, primitives and cases each ruled on. Not
yet read against the printed page; MDHC, not HVP, records exactly that.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED: cco:ont00001808 (is about). The catalogue records that this
author, at this locus, states this definition in these words; what the definition is about
is not asserted, per the neutrality invariant.

NO ENVIRONMENT ELEMENT. The published tuple has seven coordinates and no E. The revisions
manuscript revises eq. (4.1) to the eight-tuple with environment first-class — the version
the Lean development encodes — and is registered as its own bearer (mobus-book-revisions)
precisely so the revision is citable rather than silently merged. prim:environment is
deliberately NOT invoked here: the row lighting up only at the revisions entry is the census
recording the revision.

PRIMITIVES, CONSERVATIVE (ruled 2026-08-18): only the words the passage itself uses — the
Bertalanffy-1968 standard — plus time admitted notationally via Δt, the exact dQᵢ/dt
precedent. The elaborations of C, N, G, B, T and H across §4.3.3's subsections are
parts-definitions in the sense of docs/adding-an-entry.md (the Bunge Definition 1.2 rule):
they enter the scheme as primitives with their own scope notes when a parts-harvest pass
reads those subsections. Until then this census row understates Mobus deliberately, and the
understatement is recorded here rather than discovered later.

CASES: one admit. The steel-plant worked example (§4.5) is HELD, not recorded: the model
proposed it, but its licensing sentence describes the plant without ruling it a system.
It returns if a stronger licensing sentence is found in §4.5. Nothing in the chapter is
refused systemhood.

NEXT TIER: Systems/Category/ShapeMobus.lean encodes the REVISED eight-tuple (Book-revisions
Eq. 1), not this published seven-tuple. The formalisedAs pointer therefore belongs to the
forthcoming revisions entry, and this entry deliberately carries none."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Cases
##############################################################################

case:mobus-atom
    a atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:label "atoms"@en ;
    atlas:gloss "Atoms, offered as simple systems distinguished by a NULL memory element H." ;
    atlas:verbatim "Some simple systems, like atoms for example, may have a NULL H; that is there is no memory of past states and future states depend only on the current state and current inputs." ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 4, §4.3.3.5" ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; checked by Shingai Thornton (guided pass)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-18"^^xsd:date .
